<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  plMail runs on machines people own, and the platform defaults assume it does not.

  Two departures from those defaults, both narrower than they look, and neither of them the
  "trust everything" shortcut that ServerTrust exists to avoid:

  1. User CAs are trusted as well as system ones. Since Android 7 an app ignores CAs the user
     installed themselves, which is right for an app talking to a service its author operates and
     wrong here: a NAS behind a private CA the owner deliberately installed on their own phone is
     the target audience, not an attack. Note this only *widens what the platform will vouch for*.
     Everything still goes through ServerTrust, which asks the platform first and falls back to a
     pinned key only when the platform refuses — so a certificate covered by a user CA takes the
     PlatformTrusted path and never prompts, while everything else is unchanged.

  2. Cleartext is permitted. Not because it is fine, but because refusing it outright would make
     the app unusable against the emulator's http://10.0.2.2:8002 test stack and against the many
     LAN boxes running plMail with no certificate at all. The cost is paid in onboarding instead,
     which warns explicitly when the address is http:// — a decision the user can see and make,
     rather than a silent downgrade. ServerAddress.isCleartext is what that warning reads.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <!--
      Lint reads a cleartext-permitting base config as insecure, and for an app with a known server
      it is. plMail has no canonical server: the address is whatever the user typed, so there is no
      domain-config to scope this to and no list of hosts to enumerate. Suppressed here rather than
      project-wide, and paid for in onboarding, which names the risk when the address is http://.
    -->
    <base-config
        cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!--
              Lint wants this confined to <debug-overrides>, and for most apps it is right. Here it
              would break the case the app is for, so the check is suppressed on this element alone
              rather than disabled anywhere wider.

              The reason is key rotation, not convenience. ServerTrust pins the SubjectPublicKeyInfo
              — chosen because `certbot renew` keeps the key, so a ninety-day renewal does not
              re-prompt. A private CA that mints a *fresh key* on every renewal breaks that
              assumption: the pin stops matching, and PinMismatch is deliberately terminal and never
              a question, so the user is locked out of their own server by a routine renewal. Being
              able to trust the CA they installed is what keeps them on the PlatformTrusted path,
              where renewals are invisible.

              Without it the app still works against a private CA — first contact prompts with the
              fingerprint and the user pins it — so this widens nothing that TOFU had not already
              made reachable. It only stops a rotation from becoming a lockout.
            -->
            <certificates src="user" tools:ignore="AcceptsUserCertificates" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
